.lua
book.chapter(23, "Option and Result pipelines")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Choose how much failure information to retain"
.pp
Option represents absence with None or presence with Some value. Result
represents success with Ok value or failure with Err error. Both are ordinary
algebraic values. Their constructors exist before loading the portable
library, while the convenient combinators come from metalib. Choose Option
when absence needs no explanation, such as a missing index. Choose Result
when a caller needs to distinguish malformed input, unsupported data, and
other meaningful categories of failure.
.pp
An error payload need not be a string. A domain-specific ADT can preserve
structured context until the user-interface boundary renders it. Converting
every failure immediately into a sentence makes later classification harder.
Conversely, introducing a large error hierarchy for an optional lookup can
obscure a simple contract. The return type should carry the information that
clients need to make their next decision.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[positive text :=
  Int.from_string text
  |> Result.from_option "not an integer"
  |> Result.and_then (fn n -> if n > 0 then Ok n else Err "not positive")
Test.equal "success" (positive "12") (Ok 12)
Test.equal "syntax failure" (positive "bad") (Err "not an integer")
Test.equal "domain failure" (positive "0") (Err "not positive")]=],
    "Preserve failure through a validation pipeline")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Map transforms a payload; bind changes the computation"
.pp
Option.map applies a pure transformation to a present payload and preserves
None. Option.flat_map accepts a transformation that already returns Option,
avoiding a nested Option. Option.bind is the data-first counterpart. Result.map
transforms successes, map_err transforms errors, and and_then chains a
fallible computation. Read the types before choosing: mapping a fallible
function deliberately creates a nested wrapper, while chaining removes one
layer by propagating failure.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.equal "map presence" (Some 20 |> Option.map (+ 1)) (Some 21)
Test.equal "map absence" (None |> Option.map (+ 1)) None
Test.equal "chain lookup" (Some [10, 20] |> Option.flat_map (List.nth 1)) (Some 20)
Test.equal "map error" (Err 404 |> Result.map_err Int.to_string) (Err "404")
Test.equal "flatten" (Result.flatten (Ok (Ok 7))) (Ok 7)]=],
    "Operations distinguish transformation from sequencing")
.endlua
.pp
Pipeline-friendly argument order usually places the container last. Some
conveniences use another order: Option.get_or takes the option before its
default, while with_default takes the default first. Result.get_or is also
data-first. Do not infer an argument convention merely from a familiar name
in another language. Qualified names and signatures help keep a chain readable
when several related libraries use similar vocabulary.
.sh 2 "Collecting successful computations"
.pp
Sequence converts a list of wrapped values into a wrapped list. Traverse
applies a fallible function while collecting its results. Both preserve
successful input order, and empty input succeeds with an empty list. Option
traversal stops at the first None; Result traversal stops at the first Err
and returns that error. This is short-circuit validation rather than an
accumulator of every problem in the input.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.equal "option sequence" (Option.sequence [Some 1, Some 2]) (Some [1, 2])
Test.equal "empty result sequence" (Result.sequence []) (Ok [])
Test.equal "first error" (Result.sequence [Ok 1, Err "first", Err "second"])
  (Err "first")
Test.equal "parse all" (Option.traverse Int.from_string ["1", "2"]) (Some [1, 2])
Test.equal "stop on absence" (Option.traverse Int.from_string ["1", "bad", "3"]) None]=],
    "Collection traversal makes failure policy explicit")
.endlua
.pp
If every error must be reported, map a validator over the whole input and
partition or fold the results yourself. If invalid entries should be dropped,
filter_map or a constructor-pattern comprehension expresses that different
policy. None of these strategies is universally correct. A batch compiler
often wants several diagnostics, while a dependent configuration pipeline
cannot meaningfully continue until an earlier field has been validated.
.sh 2 "Eagerness still applies"
.pp
The arguments to get_or, with_default, or_else, and map2 are evaluated before
the helper runs. A present option does not prevent an expensive default
argument from being constructed. Likewise, Result.map2 cannot prevent the
work that produces its second argument. To delay that work, put it inside a
match branch or an and_then callback. Short-circuiting belongs to the callback
invocation contract, not to every expression appearing near a wrapper.
.lua
book.algorithm("Traverse with first-error semantics", {
    "Start with an empty reversed result list.",
    "For each input in source order:",
    {1, "Invoke the validator exactly when this input is reached."},
    {1, "If it returns Err, return that error immediately."},
    {1, "Otherwise prepend its payload to the accumulator."},
    "Reverse the accumulator and return Ok with the resulting list."
})
.endlua
.pp
Unwrap and get are escape hatches for established invariants. Result.unwrap
and Option.get abort on failure rather than offering recovery. Use them after
a test has explicitly established success, or inside an abstraction whose
construction proves the invariant. Sprinkling unwrap through a parser defeats
the purpose of modelling ordinary failure as data and makes error context
harder to retain.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
When translating between wrappers, account for information loss explicitly.
Option.to_result supplies an error payload for absence; Result.to_option
discards an existing error. The latter can be appropriate for a best-effort
search, but it is a poor default at a user-input boundary where the explanation
will be needed later. Nested wrappers can also be meaningful: Result (Option
value) error distinguishes a successful lookup that found nothing from a
lookup operation that failed. Flattening that distinction without a domain
reason can make recovery behavior impossible to implement correctly.
.pp
Keep these distinctions in API documentation as well as tests. A caller should
know whether None means missing data, invalid data, or an intentionally filtered
result before deciding to substitute a default.
.pp
Read metalib/option/mod.ml and metalib/result/mod.ml for the precise public
signatures and traversal order. Write two validators for a list of counts:
one that stops at the first invalid count, and one that reports every invalid
position. Test empty input and two consecutive failures. Explain which work
each version performs after the first failure, and why neither version should
silently discard an error unless its public contract explicitly says so.
